    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d35">

                <pb facs="0130" n="34" sameAs="frus:frus1921v02/pg34"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 D 811/29: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dresel</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-11-16T17:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 16,
                                1921—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 17—8:55
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>793. I today informed Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs that the American
                    Government deemed diplomatic relations might be resumed, and asked for
                    recognition provisionally as Chargé dAffaires <hi rendition="#italic">ad
                        interim</hi>. He replied that the German Government was ready to resume
                    diplomatic relations and that I am recognized in the capacity mentioned as from
                    this date.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d35-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dresel</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d35-f1" n="27" xml:id="d35-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Dresel’s nomination as Chargé, to date from Nov. 14, was confirmed by the
                    Senate Nov. 18. He presented his letter of credence as Chargé d’Affaires to the
                    German Chancellor, acting for the Minister for Foreign Affairs, Dec. 10,
                    1921.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
